1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Eight students each hold a cup of clear

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:02,800
liquid.

3
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,560
One cup is secretly different.

4
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:08,560
They share liquid with a partner, three times.

5
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:11,440
Then a drop of indicator goes into every

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:13,680
cup, and all eight turn pink.

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,440
So who started it?

8
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,640
From the final colours alone, you can't tell.

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:21,200
Let's design the model so that you can,

10
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,880
and see what it really shows about an

11
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:23,680
epidemic.

12
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:27,620
An epidemic is a rise in cases above

13
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,660
what's expected in a population.

14
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:32,420
This is a model of one: a simplified

15
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:33,860
version of something real.

16
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:35,940
Each cup is a person.

17
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,060
Mixing liquid with a partner is a contact,

18
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:40,420
like direct contact.

19
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:43,860
The secret cup holds a dilute base, sodium

20
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:46,660
carbonate solution, which stands for a pathogen.

21
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:49,220
And the indicator is the test.

22
00:00:50,434 --> 00:00:52,833
The marker has to stay invisible until you

23
00:00:52,914 --> 00:00:55,873
test, so nobody knows who's infected while they

24
00:00:56,114 --> 00:00:56,633
mix.

25
00:00:56,914 --> 00:01:00,433
Phenolphthalein is an indicator, a chemical that changes

26
00:01:00,594 --> 00:01:01,313
colour.

27
00:01:01,394 --> 00:01:04,193
It stays colourless in water, but turns pink

28
00:01:04,234 --> 00:01:06,433
in a solution that's basic enough.

29
00:01:06,594 --> 00:01:08,993
So a cup that's received enough base turns

30
00:01:09,154 --> 00:01:11,793
pink, and a cup that hasn't stays clear.

31
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,720
Everyone starts with ten millilitres in a numbered

32
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:18,840
cup, and your teacher secretly makes one cup

33
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:19,640
the starter.

34
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,280
Each round, find a new partner.

35
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:24,680
One of you pours all your liquid into

36
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,520
the other's cup, then pours half back.

37
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,840
Before round one, and after every round, put

38
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:33,080
three drops from your cup into your own

39
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:34,760
column of a well plate.

40
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,080
And record each partner on the class contact

41
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:37,880
sheet.

42
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:41,080
Keep volumes, pours and drops the same, so

43
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:43,640
only the contacts decide who turns pink.

44
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:46,546
You need two controls.

45
00:01:46,787 --> 00:01:48,626
The negative control is a cup of the

46
00:01:48,787 --> 00:01:50,946
same water that nobody mixes.

47
00:01:51,427 --> 00:01:54,146
With indicator it should stay clear, which shows

48
00:01:54,187 --> 00:01:56,786
the water and cups weren't already basic.

49
00:01:57,027 --> 00:01:58,786
The positive control is a cup of the

50
00:01:58,867 --> 00:02:01,906
base, which should turn pink, showing the indicator

51
00:02:01,987 --> 00:02:02,466
works.

52
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:05,426
The teacher's secret record checks your tracing is

53
00:02:05,507 --> 00:02:06,066
accurate.

54
00:02:06,467 --> 00:02:08,866
Then repeat with a new starter: if it

55
00:02:08,947 --> 00:02:11,746
finds the source every time, it's reliable.

56
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:15,446
After round three, your teacher adds a drop

57
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:17,286
of indicator to every cup.

58
00:02:17,767 --> 00:02:19,366
All eight turn pink.

59
00:02:19,927 --> 00:02:22,806
The final test shows who's infected now, but

60
00:02:22,887 --> 00:02:25,446
not who was first, or when anyone caught

61
00:02:25,527 --> 00:02:25,766
it.

62
00:02:26,247 --> 00:02:28,406
That's why the saved samples matter.

63
00:02:29,634 --> 00:02:31,953
Now the teacher adds indicator to the well

64
00:02:32,154 --> 00:02:32,633
plate.

65
00:02:32,914 --> 00:02:34,353
Read it row by row.

66
00:02:34,914 --> 00:02:37,713
At the start, only D is pink, so

67
00:02:37,954 --> 00:02:39,073
D was the starter.

68
00:02:39,714 --> 00:02:42,593
After round one, C is pink too, and

69
00:02:42,674 --> 00:02:45,673
the contact sheet shows C mixed with D.

70
00:02:46,034 --> 00:02:48,513
Without the start row, you couldn't tell C

71
00:02:48,594 --> 00:02:49,513
from D.

72
00:02:49,954 --> 00:02:53,073
After round two, A and G join them,

73
00:02:53,314 --> 00:02:54,873
partners of C and D.

74
00:02:55,314 --> 00:02:58,273
Real outbreaks have no start sample, so health

75
00:02:58,354 --> 00:03:01,473
teams use when symptoms began, and who each

76
00:03:01,554 --> 00:03:04,033
case met, called contact tracing.

77
00:03:05,234 --> 00:03:07,953
Now count the new cases each round: one

78
00:03:07,994 --> 00:03:10,833
at the start, one after round one, two

79
00:03:10,914 --> 00:03:14,153
after round two, four after round three.

80
00:03:14,434 --> 00:03:16,913
Plotted against time as a bar graph, that's

81
00:03:16,994 --> 00:03:20,593
an epidemic curve: new cases against time.

82
00:03:20,834 --> 00:03:23,873
Here every infected cup met a clear one,

83
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:27,233
so the number infected doubled each round: one,

84
00:03:27,394 --> 00:03:29,073
two, four, eight.

85
00:03:29,314 --> 00:03:32,713
That growing shape is typical of person-to-person spread.

86
00:03:32,914 --> 00:03:34,753
If all eight cups had been filled from

87
00:03:34,834 --> 00:03:37,633
one contaminated jug, they'd all turn pink at

88
00:03:37,714 --> 00:03:39,873
once: one sharp peak.

89
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:43,660
You can model indirect contact too.

90
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,060
Dab a little fluorescent powder on a door

91
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:48,540
handle, a shared object or fomite.

92
00:03:49,180 --> 00:03:52,220
After a lesson, shine an ultraviolet torch around

93
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:52,780
the room.

94
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:56,380
Glowing specks show where the powder travelled: other

95
00:03:56,460 --> 00:03:58,460
handles, desks and pens.

96
00:03:59,180 --> 00:04:01,900
Vectors are harder to model safely, so use

97
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:03,340
secondary data for them.

98
00:04:04,567 --> 00:04:05,846
Now the limits.

99
00:04:06,087 --> 00:04:09,126
In the model, every contact passes it on,

100
00:04:09,207 --> 00:04:11,846
but real contacts don't always transmit.

101
00:04:12,327 --> 00:04:15,766
Real pathogens multiply inside a host, but here

102
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:18,486
the base gets more dilute with each pour,

103
00:04:18,567 --> 00:04:20,886
so after many rounds the pink could fade

104
00:04:20,967 --> 00:04:22,246
and miss a case.

105
00:04:22,287 --> 00:04:24,406
There's no delay before someone can pass it

106
00:04:24,487 --> 00:04:28,086
on, no recovery and no immunity, and everyone

107
00:04:28,167 --> 00:04:31,046
meets exactly one person per round.

108
00:04:31,167 --> 00:04:33,046
So the model is valid for showing how

109
00:04:33,127 --> 00:04:36,566
contacts link cases, not for predicting how fast

110
00:04:36,647 --> 00:04:38,086
a real disease spreads.

111
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:40,420
The risk assessment.

112
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:45,140
Sodium carbonate solution can irritate eyes, and phenolphthalein

113
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:48,660
indicator is flammable and may cause cancer.

114
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,780
So wear safety glasses, let your teacher add

115
00:04:51,820 --> 00:04:54,420
the indicator, and keep flames away.

116
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:57,700
Never taste or drink anything: these are lab

117
00:04:57,780 --> 00:04:58,740
cups.

118
00:04:58,780 --> 00:05:02,020
Don't shine the ultraviolet torch at anyone's eyes.

119
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:04,900
Wipe up spills, wash your hands, and dispose

120
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:06,900
of liquids as your teacher directs.

121
00:05:08,100 --> 00:05:09,060
Your turn.

122
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:11,860
Six students ran the model for two rounds.

123
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:15,140
In round one, P mixed with Q, R

124
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:17,140
with S, and T with U.

125
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:20,740
In round two, P mixed with R, Q

126
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:22,900
with T, and S with U.

127
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:26,180
At the end, P, Q, R and T

128
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:26,900
were pink.

129
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:30,420
Identify which student or students could have been

130
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:32,980
the starter, and justify your answer.

131
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,860
Then propose one change to the method that

132
00:05:35,940 --> 00:05:37,580
would identify the starter.

133
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,140
Pause here to answer.

134
00:05:49,567 --> 00:05:51,646
Only P or Q fits.

135
00:05:52,047 --> 00:05:54,526
If P started, Q catches it in round

136
00:05:54,607 --> 00:05:57,166
one, then P passes it to R, and

137
00:05:57,247 --> 00:05:59,726
Q to T, while S and U stay

138
00:05:59,807 --> 00:06:00,206
clear.

139
00:06:00,847 --> 00:06:03,086
Starting with Q gives the same result.

140
00:06:03,487 --> 00:06:05,646
R or T would have infected S or

141
00:06:05,727 --> 00:06:06,126
U.

142
00:06:06,607 --> 00:06:08,686
You can't tell P from Q, because they

143
00:06:08,767 --> 00:06:11,886
mixed first, so test a sample saved before

144
00:06:11,967 --> 00:06:12,606
round one.

145
00:06:13,167 --> 00:06:15,966
Saying P, because P is listed first, is

146
00:06:16,007 --> 00:06:17,406
the classic mistake.

147
00:06:18,634 --> 00:06:20,473
A class of eight ran the model for

148
00:06:20,554 --> 00:06:23,673
three rounds, and all eight cups turned pink.

149
00:06:24,234 --> 00:06:26,633
A student wrote, "The model shows that a

150
00:06:26,714 --> 00:06:29,193
real disease would infect everyone in a class

151
00:06:29,394 --> 00:06:33,033
within three rounds of contact." Assess this claim.

152
00:06:33,194 --> 00:06:34,393
Pause here to answer.

153
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,480
The claim overreaches.

154
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:50,320
In the model, every contact transmits, so cases

155
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:51,360
climb fast.

156
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:54,880
Real contacts don't always pass on a pathogen,

157
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,360
some people are immune, and there's a delay

158
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:00,000
before people can spread it, so real spread

159
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:02,160
depends on the disease and the group.

160
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:05,280
The model does show how fast linked contacts

161
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:06,240
can multiply.

162
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,520
Calling it useless because it uses chemicals, not

163
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:11,840
germs, is the classic mistake.

164
00:07:13,067 --> 00:07:17,946
So: a hidden marker, both controls, recorded contacts,

165
00:07:18,027 --> 00:07:21,866
a sample saved every round, and repeat runs.

166
00:07:21,947 --> 00:07:24,906
The last test shows who, the saved samples

167
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:28,186
show when, and the contacts show how.

168
00:07:28,387 --> 00:07:31,146
Claim only what the model can show.

169
00:07:31,227 --> 00:07:34,106
Next: testing a water or food sample for

170
00:07:34,187 --> 00:07:35,066
microbes.

171
00:07:36,267 --> 00:07:37,706
Here are the key notes.

172
00:07:37,947 --> 00:07:39,866
Pause the video if you need more time

173
00:07:39,947 --> 00:07:40,746
to copy them.
